{
  "name": "How to Identify Hidden Trip Hazards in Your Home",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Finding trip hazards isn't always obvious; sometimes, they're hiding in plain sight! A great way to start is with a thorough walkthrough inspection of your home. Focus on high-traffic areas and any spots where you or family members tend to move quickly. Document what you find, noting the exact location and how severe the hazard seems. Look for those curled edges on area rugs and mats, loose carpeting that buckles, or even uneven transitions between different types of flooring. Don't forget about poorly lit areas like hallways, staircases, or entryways, as these can make even minor obstacles dangerous. Also, keep an eye out for any cords or cables that cross walkways. Once you’ve identified potential hazards, simple fixes can often do the trick, like securing rugs with non-slip backing or rearranging furniture to get cords out of the way. But when you’re dealing with things like damaged flooring or significant uneven transitions, professional help might be the best route to ensure a permanent and safe solution. Taking the time to really look can prevent a nasty fall.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
